7084
08:12:53,010 --> 08:12:56,050
For some, it consolidates political control.

7085
08:12:56,050 --> 08:12:59,370
For others, it opens new streams of profit.

7086
08:12:59,370 --> 08:13:05,450
And for many, it creates a constant state of threat that justifies its own continuation.

7087
08:13:05,450 --> 08:13:07,770
Wars are not always designed to end.

7088
08:13:07,770 --> 08:13:13,630
They evolve, adapt and sometimes they sustain the very actors fighting them.

7089
08:13:13,630 --> 08:13:17,230
In the Middle East, that system is becoming increasingly fragmented

7090
08:13:17,230 --> 08:13:20,150
as states are no longer the only players.

7091
08:13:20,150 --> 08:13:25,350
Armed groups, informal networks and regional actors play roles that shape conflicts

7092
08:13:25,350 --> 08:13:28,590
in ways the global powers can't fully control.

7093
08:13:28,590 --> 08:13:31,830
The consequences stretch far beyond the battlefield.

7094
08:13:31,830 --> 08:13:36,190
From energy markets to inflation, from superpowers to developing economies,

7095
08:13:36,190 --> 08:13:38,710
the costs of conflict are global.

7096
08:13:38,710 --> 08:13:41,110
But so too are its incentives.

7097
08:13:41,110 --> 08:13:44,830
So if war can become economically and politically useful,

7098
08:13:44,830 --> 08:13:46,670
what does that mean for peace?

7099
08:13:46,670 --> 08:13:50,910
To make sense of it all and to understand why some wars don't end,

7100
08:13:50,910 --> 08:13:54,710
we turn to a leading voice in the political economy of conflict.

7101
08:13:54,710 --> 08:13:57,990
Professor of Conflict Studies at the London School of Economics,

7102
08:13:57,990 --> 08:14:05,180
David Keane, talks to Al Jazeera.

7103
08:14:05,180 --> 08:14:07,820
David Keane, welcome to Talk to Al Jazeera.

7104
08:14:07,820 --> 08:14:12,380
You are a pioneer in the political economy of conflict,

7105
08:14:12,380 --> 08:14:16,900
so a field that shifts the focus away from who may be winning the war militarily

7106
08:14:16,900 --> 08:14:22,420
to who is essentially benefiting politically from all of this.

7107
08:14:22,420 --> 08:14:25,100
Can you start by explaining to us what that means?

7108
08:14:25,100 --> 08:14:28,300
Set out your store, friends, if you may.

7109
08:14:28,300 --> 08:14:34,520
Well, I think if we think about the aims in a war,

7110
08:14:34,520 --> 08:14:38,270
we often think the aim in a war is to win.

7111
08:14:38,270 --> 08:14:42,350
And clearly, that is an important aim in a war.

7112
08:14:42,350 --> 08:14:44,670
But there are also many others.

7113
08:14:44,670 --> 08:14:47,150
And one of them, I think, is making money.

7114
08:14:47,390 --> 08:14:53,390
Another one is a sort of a set of political functions or benefits

7115
08:14:53,390 --> 08:14:59,590
that may include things like suppressing dissent under the cover of war,

7116
08:14:59,590 --> 08:15:06,180
or divide and rule, or painting dissent as disloyalty.

7117
08:15:06,180 --> 08:15:10,980
Or even turning your enemy into the image

7118
08:15:10,980 --> 08:15:13,380
that you've put about in your propaganda,

7119
08:15:13,380 --> 08:15:17,650
making the enemy resemble your propaganda.

7120
08:15:17,650 --> 08:15:19,530
So those are two other aims.

7121
08:15:19,530 --> 08:15:21,490
Then there are sort of psychological aims.

7122
08:15:21,490 --> 08:15:25,810
And I think reversing shame and humiliation is often a big one.

7123
08:15:25,810 --> 08:15:31,090
And a fourth one, I would say, is the simple one of trying to stay alive.

7124
08:15:31,090 --> 08:15:34,930
Now, if the aim is winning, then what you need to do

7125
08:15:34,930 --> 08:15:38,570
is weaken or eliminate your enemy.

7126
08:15:38,570 --> 08:15:40,890
But with a lot of the other aims,

7127
08:15:40,930 --> 08:15:45,410
sometimes the enemy can be surprisingly useful.

7128
08:15:45,410 --> 08:15:48,250
So we get this phenomenon of useful enemies,

7129
08:15:48,250 --> 08:15:51,330
which I think is a real phenomenon.

7130
08:15:51,330 --> 08:15:55,970
And the problem there is that people acquire, as it were,

7131
08:15:55,970 --> 08:15:59,610
like quite a wide range of vested interests,

7132
08:15:59,610 --> 08:16:04,090
acquire an interest sometimes in actually maintaining the enemy

7133
08:16:04,090 --> 08:16:09,610
or taking actions that are predictably counterproductive

7134
08:16:09,610 --> 08:16:12,930
in terms of the reproduction of the enemy.

7135
08:16:12,930 --> 08:16:18,490
So making people angry, attacking civilians,

7136
08:16:18,490 --> 08:16:22,450
winning, as it were, new recruits for the entity.

7137
08:16:22,450 --> 08:16:26,930
It might be a rebel group or a terrorist group or a nation

7138
08:16:26,930 --> 08:16:32,090
that they claim to be trying to oppose or weaken.

7139
08:16:32,090 --> 08:16:35,450
So there's a question mark I'm trying to raise in a way

7140
08:16:35,450 --> 08:16:37,530
about the aims in a war

7141
08:16:37,570 --> 08:16:42,610
and whether the enemy is somebody that you want to eliminate

7142
08:16:42,610 --> 08:16:46,370
or somebody that you might be interested at some level

7143
08:16:46,370 --> 08:16:49,890
in continuing the existence of.

7144
08:16:49,890 --> 08:16:53,850
David, at this juncture in the Israeli-US war on Iran,

7145
08:16:53,850 --> 08:16:56,290
where despite claims by both sides

7146
08:16:56,290 --> 08:16:58,650
of a degree of military success,

7147
08:16:58,650 --> 08:17:03,850
it's almost impossible to say who the actual winners are.

7148
08:17:03,850 --> 08:17:08,970
But given your focus on how you frame world conflict,

7149
08:17:08,970 --> 08:17:11,290
I wonder whether it's possible to ask

7150
08:17:11,290 --> 08:17:14,250
who benefits from this tension the most

7151
08:17:14,250 --> 08:17:18,530
without potentially the need for an absolute victory?

7152
08:17:18,530 --> 08:17:20,570
Yeah, I mean, I think it's an interesting

7153
08:17:20,570 --> 08:17:22,810
and complicated question.

7154
08:17:22,810 --> 08:17:25,730
I mean, one of the things that we can observe,

7155
08:17:25,730 --> 08:17:28,490
and this is, I think, very common in wars,

7156
08:17:28,490 --> 08:17:31,450
is that even when there hasn't really been

7157
08:17:31,890 --> 08:17:36,370
convincing victory by any party,

7158
08:17:36,370 --> 08:17:40,010
a number of different parties find reasons to claim

7159
08:17:41,170 --> 08:17:45,460
that they have registered a success in the war.

7160
08:17:45,460 --> 08:17:49,260
And Trump, I think, awarded himself 15 out of 10

7161
08:17:49,260 --> 08:17:51,820
for his success in the war.

7162
08:17:51,820 --> 08:17:56,660
Israel, obviously, would claim some security benefits.

7163
08:17:56,660 --> 08:18:01,660
Iran can point to its ability to retaliate and so on.

7164
08:18:01,900 --> 08:18:04,380
But I think if you can claim success,

7165
08:18:04,380 --> 08:18:08,620
then that is kind of a political benefit in itself.

7166
08:18:09,900 --> 08:18:14,900
Beyond that, I think that the functions of war

7167
08:18:15,420 --> 08:18:18,980
as a distraction and the functions of war

7168
08:18:18,980 --> 08:18:23,980
as something that can quiet an internal dissent

7169
08:18:24,260 --> 08:18:28,940
and allow you to paint opponents as disloyal

7170
08:18:28,940 --> 08:18:32,140
is something that is really quite important

7171
08:18:32,140 --> 08:18:37,140
in the United States, in Iran, and in Israel.

7172
08:18:37,500 --> 08:18:40,900
And I think there are elements, for example,

7173
08:18:40,900 --> 08:18:45,580
of the revolutionary guards in Iran,

7174
08:18:45,580 --> 08:18:49,260
that have an interest in the continuation of sanctions,

7175
08:18:49,260 --> 08:18:52,340
that have an interest in the continuation

7176
08:18:52,340 --> 08:18:57,340
of international tension, pointing to common enemies,

7177
08:18:57,740 --> 08:19:01,700
labeling dissenters as disloyal and so on.

7178
08:19:01,700 --> 08:19:05,620
So these things come into play.

7179
08:19:05,620 --> 08:19:09,020
And I think in terms of Israeli politics,

7180
08:19:09,020 --> 08:19:13,940
there's been a lot of trying to portray opposition

7181
08:19:13,940 --> 08:19:17,460
as disloyal, there's been attempts to postpone

7182
08:19:17,460 --> 08:19:19,900
accountability for corruption.

7183
08:19:19,940 --> 08:19:24,460
There is, in a way, I think, a serial use of war,

7184
08:19:24,460 --> 08:19:29,460
including in Gaza, which was really a genocide in my book

7185
08:19:31,900 --> 08:19:36,900
to distract from a process of accountability at home.

7186
08:19:36,900 --> 08:19:39,020
I think Trump himself is very concerned

7187
08:19:39,020 --> 08:19:40,740
with distracting people at the moment.

7188
08:19:40,740 --> 08:19:42,380
Yeah, let me just jump in there

7189
08:19:42,380 --> 08:19:45,020
and break down what you've just said

7190
08:19:45,020 --> 08:19:46,940
because you've made it sound as if

7191
08:19:47,700 --> 08:19:52,180
the benefits of conflict for some countries

7192
08:19:52,180 --> 08:19:56,540
far outweigh the desire for any kind of lasting resolution

7193
08:19:56,540 --> 08:20:00,100
as a means of controlling opposition for...

7194
08:20:00,000 --> 08:20:04,000
or any kind of opposition within those given countries.

7195
08:20:04,000 --> 08:20:06,000
So war is the ultimate distraction, as you say.

7196
08:20:06,000 --> 08:20:08,260
Well, it is the ultimate distraction.

7197
08:20:08,260 --> 08:20:11,260
It doesn't mean that some of the threats are not real, you know,

7198
08:20:11,260 --> 08:20:13,260
because 9-11 was very real.

7199
08:20:13,260 --> 08:20:19,260
Iran has made many threats against Israel and so on.

7200
08:20:19,260 --> 08:20:24,260
You know, we have the horrific Hamas attacks on Israel and so on.

7201
08:20:24,260 --> 08:20:28,260
So these things are not necessarily invented

7202
08:20:28,260 --> 08:20:32,580
and often they're too ultra-real and all too horrible.

7203
08:20:32,580 --> 08:20:39,580
But nevertheless, you know, if we look at the pattern of responding to some of these events,

7204
08:20:39,580 --> 08:20:43,580
I think it does predictably create new enemies.

7205
08:20:43,580 --> 08:20:47,580
It does predictably enrage people.

7206
08:20:47,580 --> 08:20:53,580
And so we have to look beyond the declared aim of weakening the enemy

7207
08:20:53,580 --> 08:20:59,580
and the other possible functions, psychological, economic and political,

7208
08:20:59,580 --> 08:21:04,580
of taking these, what in my book, are predictably counterproductive actions

7209
08:21:04,580 --> 08:21:10,580
that are going to exacerbate conflict, that are going to perpetuate the war on terror,

7210
08:21:10,580 --> 08:21:16,580
probably entrenching the revolutionary guards in Iran, though, of course, we don't know.

7211
08:21:16,580 --> 08:21:19,580
You know, we have to keep an open mind about these things.

7212
08:21:19,580 --> 08:21:21,580
Just picking up David on something else you said,

7213
08:21:21,580 --> 08:21:26,580
because I'd like to know how this narrative of, I guess, a kind of continuous existential threat

7214
08:21:26,580 --> 08:21:32,580
allows countries to bypass demands within their own country.

7215
08:21:32,580 --> 08:21:36,580
So let's say demands for economic reform, human rights,

7216
08:21:36,580 --> 08:21:39,580
desire for an increase in public spending,

7217
08:21:39,580 --> 08:21:45,580
because in the first 100 hours of this conflict, the U.S. burned through $800 million per day.

7218
08:21:45,580 --> 08:21:50,580
I mean, that's significantly higher than the opening of the Iraq or Afghanistan wars.

7219
08:21:50,580 --> 08:21:54,580
I mean, that's money that could have been spent on housing, on education,

7220
08:21:54,580 --> 08:22:01,700
on healthcare in the United States, but it's been funneled into the war coffers.

7221
08:22:01,700 --> 08:22:08,310
Yeah, and I actually thought, you know, during the Trump's first administration,

7222
08:22:08,310 --> 08:22:16,530
the one sort of good thing about him was that he seemed to be relatively reluctant

7223
08:22:16,530 --> 08:22:38,460
to launch international wars.

7224
08:22:38,460 --> 08:22:43,650
You know, and he had a whole critique of the neoconservatives

7225
08:22:43,650 --> 08:22:49,160
and the way that, you know, a lot of the elements of the war on terror,

7226
08:22:49,160 --> 08:22:54,160
the waste of money, the waste of lives and so on, that that involved.

7227
08:22:54,160 --> 08:23:01,160
And that to me was the one really relatively convincing part of his campaign,

7228
08:23:01,160 --> 08:23:05,160
but he now seems to have radically departed from that.

7229
08:23:05,160 --> 08:23:08,160
And of course, Trump loves freedom.

7230
08:23:08,160 --> 08:23:15,160
I mean, he loves his own freedom and the ability to change his mind, to set the agenda.

7231
08:23:15,160 --> 08:23:20,160
And he has taken the United States in a radically different direction.

7232
08:23:20,160 --> 08:23:29,160
I also think Israel has kind of adopted some increasingly drastic remedies

7233
08:23:29,160 --> 08:23:36,160
to trying to solve the problem of its own security, you know, as we saw in Gaza.

7234
08:23:36,160 --> 08:23:39,160
So these things can kind of radicalize.

7235
08:23:39,160 --> 08:23:48,510
And actually, I think if you create enough enemies or you contribute to the existence of another enemies,

7236
08:23:48,510 --> 08:23:55,670
that in itself can encourage some very drastic solutions because of the fear of retaliation.

7237
08:23:55,670 --> 08:23:57,670
Talking about drastic solutions.

7238
08:23:57,670 --> 08:24:06,670
This is what the US Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth said when he was questioned about the eye-watering costs of waging this war.

7239
08:24:06,670 --> 08:24:10,670
He said, quote, it costs a lot of money to kill bad guys.

7240
08:24:10,670 --> 08:24:13,670
It costs a lot of money to kill bad guys.

7241
08:24:13,670 --> 08:24:19,670
I mean, ironically, the Iranians have shown that they can fight an asymmetric war at a fraction of the cost.

7242
08:24:19,670 --> 08:24:22,670
That must be a rude awakening for the Trump administration.

7243
08:24:22,670 --> 08:24:39,800
Yeah, I think the Iranian regime has been quite clever in promoting influence in places like Syria, in Lebanon and so on at a fairly low cost.

7244
08:24:39,800 --> 08:24:50,800
You know, and a lot of it is encouraging militia strategies and recruiting people who are often quite impoverished at relatively low costs,

7245
08:24:50,800 --> 08:24:54,800
providing training which is relatively cheap and so on.

7246
08:24:54,800 --> 08:25:08,800
As far as the United States government is concerned, I think this division of people into good guys and bad guys is incredibly simplistic.

7247
08:25:08,800 --> 08:25:12,800
And it goes back at least as far as the Vietnam War.

7248
08:25:12,800 --> 08:25:24,800
And the idea there, as you know, was if we can just kill enough bad guys, kill enough Viet Cong, then this is going to solve the problem of Vietnam.

7249
08:25:24,800 --> 08:25:34,800
But we know that the mass bombing of Vietnam created a huge number of additional recruits to the Viet Cong.

7250
08:25:34,800 --> 08:25:37,800
So those tactics don't work.

7251
08:25:37,800 --> 08:25:44,800
And you have to think more creatively about how to isolate the so-called bad guys.

7252
08:25:44,800 --> 08:25:55,800
And that usually means tackling the corruption and the violence that has created a rebellion or a terrorist movement in the first place.

7253
08:25:55,800 --> 08:26:07,800
On that point, David, let's focus on Iran, if we may, because there are those clearly, as you've suggested, who benefit, I guess, from a continuation of the current system in Iran.

7254
08:26:07,800 --> 08:26:14,800
And when I say current system, I also mean potentially benefiting from a continuation of Western sanctions also.

7255
08:26:14,800 --> 08:26:21,800
I'm thinking about those who may have benefited from state monopolies, those who are controlling, smuggling, or the black markets, for instance.

7256
08:26:21,800 --> 08:26:26,960
Well, yeah, this is something that we see many times in conflicts.

7257
08:26:26,960 --> 08:26:29,960
We saw it in Syria.

7258
08:26:29,960 --> 08:26:35,960
We've seen it in former Yugoslavia as well, and Iraq as well.

7259
08:26:35,960 --> 08:26:46,960
When you get sanctions, you tend to get a big price difference between the country that's being sanctioned and the surrounding area.

7260
08:26:46,960 --> 08:26:56,960
And if you can actually successfully breach international sanctions, which clearly the revolutionary guards have done in the case of Iran,

7261
08:26:56,960 --> 08:27:08,960
the profits are often very considerable, and often you get a monopoly, as they have had, basically, as I understand it, on the smuggling that is breaching the sanctions.

7262
08:27:08,960 --> 08:27:17,960
So I do think sanctions are very problematic in that way, and it partly relates to people's ability to get around them.

7263
08:27:17,960 --> 08:27:28,990
And of course, the diverse nature of superpowers at the moment with Russia and China, and so on, you have plenty of options.

7264
08:27:28,990 --> 08:27:32,250
And you can go outside the dollar-based system.

7265
08:27:32,250 --> 08:27:36,250
This is part of the reason why so many people want gold at the moment.

7266
08:27:36,250 --> 08:27:40,250
Let me try to evade some of these sanctions.

7267
08:27:40,250 --> 08:27:48,250
Let me try and distill this then, because what I'm hearing is that you're saying that sometimes when the cost of war is cheaper for a nation,

7268
08:27:48,250 --> 08:27:58,250
or more beneficial for a nation than the price of peace, that it works in their interest to, in some way, sustain frictions, to maintain hostilities.

7269
08:27:58,250 --> 08:28:12,540
Yeah, I mean, I think the strongest example of that, or one of the strongest, is basically the incredible level of military spending within the United States that survived the end of the Cold War.

7270
08:28:12,540 --> 08:28:26,630
You know, you would have thought that was an opportunity to dismantle the military industrial structures, but spending stayed pretty much at the levels of the height of the Cold War.

7271
08:28:26,630 --> 08:28:29,630
So why was that opportunity not taken?

7272
08:28:29,630 --> 08:28:37,630
Well, clearly, there were a number of threats around the world, which the government pointed to, and the defense industry pointed to,

7273
08:28:37,630 --> 08:28:44,630
and terrorism was foremost among them for a while, and then Russia kind of came up with the agenda.

7274
08:28:44,630 --> 08:28:48,630
But that was an opportunity that was spurned.

7275
08:28:49,630 --> 08:28:57,730
And of course, these things have consequences, because if you build up your own arms, you're encouraging the other guy to build up their arms,

7276
08:28:57,730 --> 08:29:02,730
and then you can point to his arms build up as justification for your own.

7277
08:29:02,730 --> 08:29:06,020
And this is what happened during the Cold War.

7278
08:29:06,020 --> 08:29:14,020
So we have these kind of self-sustaining systems that are built on the mass manufacture of things that can kill people.

7279
08:29:14,020 --> 08:29:16,020
It is extremely profitable.

7280
08:29:16,020 --> 08:29:25,110
And there are a lot of predictably counterproductive tactics that are being adopted, which serve those systems.

7281
08:29:25,110 --> 08:29:28,110
And I think very rarely work.

7282
08:29:28,110 --> 08:29:30,110
Of course, you can declare victory.

7283
08:29:30,110 --> 08:29:38,240
You know, remember the mission accomplished in 2003 that George W. Bush declared.

7284
08:29:38,240 --> 08:29:48,240
But these victories very rarely materialize, because a war economy will often deepen, it will complicate.

7285
08:29:48,240 --> 08:29:57,240
Let me pick you up upon that point of mission accomplished, because we're talking about the apparent end of the war on terror,

7286
08:29:57,240 --> 08:30:02,240
the decades of conflict that came after 9-11.

7287
08:30:02,240 --> 08:30:06,240
I mean, we did, of course, see the destruction of Al Qaeda.

7288
08:30:06,240 --> 08:30:09,240
We saw the killing of Osama bin Laden.

7289
08:30:09,240 --> 08:30:12,240
We saw the U.S. pull out of Afghanistan.

7290
08:30:12,240 --> 08:30:19,240
But in your mind, is what we are seeing now in the Middle East some way of a continuation of that war on terror?

7291
08:30:19,240 --> 08:30:21,240
Or is it something else?

7292
08:30:21,240 --> 08:30:23,720
Is it something new?

7293
08:30:23,720 --> 08:30:27,720
Well, I think it is partly a continuation of it.

7294
08:30:28,720 --> 08:30:33,720
We saw, as you say, the defeat of Al Qaeda.

7295
08:30:33,720 --> 08:30:38,720
But then, of course, we had Al Qaeda in Iraq, which kind of grew out of it.

7296
08:30:38,720 --> 08:30:42,720
There was a claim of victory against Al Qaeda in Iraq.

7297
08:30:42,720 --> 08:30:49,720
But then we had ISIS, which grew to a large extent out of Al Qaeda in Iraq.

7298
08:30:49,720 --> 08:30:56,720
So you have this sort of serial declaration of victories, and then people can relax for a little bit.

7299
08:30:56,720 --> 08:30:58,720
But then something else happens.

7300
08:30:58,720 --> 08:31:01,720
And a lot of times these threats are feeding into each other.

7301
08:31:01,720 --> 08:31:05,720
There's a continuity between these organizations.

7302
08:31:05,720 --> 08:31:13,720
I think the destruction of Gaza, that is, in a way, a very distinct problem.

7303
08:31:13,720 --> 08:31:20,720
But it was an action that was presented by Israel within the framework of a war on terror.

7304
08:31:21,720 --> 08:31:30,720
In Sri Lanka, where I was in 2009, the government wreaked horrible destruction on the Tamil population

7305
08:31:30,720 --> 08:31:33,720
in the name of an international war on terror.

7306
08:31:33,720 --> 08:31:38,720
And it was actually difficult for Britain or the United States to criticize that.

7307
08:31:38,720 --> 08:31:43,720
When they themselves were detaining people indefinitely,

7308
08:31:43,720 --> 08:31:48,720
they themselves had the language of the war on terror and we need to be ruthless.

7309
08:31:48,720 --> 08:31:53,720
We need to suspend liberty to a certain extent in the interest of security.

7310
08:31:53,720 --> 08:31:56,720
Specifically on the language used around the war on terror,

7311
08:31:56,720 --> 08:32:04,720
I guess a very seismic symbolic shift at least came with the rebranding of the Secretary of Defense

7312
08:32:04,720 --> 08:32:09,720
to the Secretary of War, the Defense Department to the War Department

7313
08:32:09,720 --> 08:32:15,720
because I mean that signaled almost a kind of move away from more of a reactive war on terror

7314
08:32:15,720 --> 08:32:21,720
to a US administration that sought to project power, to pursue a peace through strength,

7315
08:32:21,720 --> 08:32:25,720
I think, as the words used by Pete Hegseth.

7316
08:32:25,720 --> 08:32:29,720
So do you see that as a rebranding of what's gone before?

7317
08:32:29,720 --> 08:32:33,720
Or is that really a defining departure from what we've seen in the past?

7318
08:32:33,720 --> 08:32:38,880
I think it's a great question. I think it is partly a rebranding.

7319
08:32:38,880 --> 08:32:48,880
But it's partly an indication that violence and the assertion of America's right,

7320
08:32:48,880 --> 08:32:57,880
in particular, to do what it likes, has become sort of incredibly shameless under Trump.

7321
08:32:57,880 --> 08:33:03,880
And Trump is somebody really who has systematically rejected shame

7322
08:33:03,880 --> 08:33:07,880
and indeed made a lot of political capital, in my opinion,

7323
08:33:07,880 --> 08:33:14,880
out of offering people a kind of vicarious shamelessness through his abuses

7324
08:33:14,880 --> 08:33:22,880
and through his spectacular demonstrations of freedom, as I say, freedom for himself.

7325
08:33:22,880 --> 08:33:26,880
So I think there are some significant differences.

7326
08:33:26,880 --> 08:33:29,880
For example, George W. Bush and Tony Blair,

7327
08:33:29,880 --> 08:33:36,880
they did make some efforts to try to create some sense of international legitimacy

7328
08:33:36,880 --> 08:33:43,880
for what they wanted to do in Iraq and to try and get the UN on board to a certain extent.

7329
08:33:43,880 --> 08:33:48,880
We're not seeing that now with Trump and Netanyahu.

7330
08:33:48,880 --> 08:33:54,880
We're just seeing a kind of go it alone spirit, which I think is incredibly dangerous

7331
08:33:54,880 --> 08:33:57,880
and incredibly destructive.

7332
08:33:57,880 --> 08:34:01,880
So I do think there's a shamelessness about what's going on

7333
08:34:01,880 --> 08:34:06,880
and that thing you mentioned about relabeling as a Department of War.

7334
08:34:06,880 --> 08:34:10,040
You know, Trump would say, well, this is, you know, this is honesty.

7335
08:34:10,040 --> 08:34:14,040
You know, he projects this image of honesty. I'm not a hypocrite.

7336
08:34:14,040 --> 08:34:18,040
The liberals are hypocrites. I tell you the truth as it is.

7337
08:34:18,040 --> 08:34:23,040
And he's made some considerable political capital out of that, in my opinion.

7338
08:34:23,040 --> 08:34:26,040
But it's also a very frightening turn.

7339
08:34:26,040 --> 08:34:29,040
David, talking about political capital, we've talked about the big powers.

7340
08:34:29,040 --> 08:34:32,040
I'd like to talk about the small powers, if I may,

7341
08:34:32,040 --> 08:34:35,040
because I wonder if the rise of non-state actors,

7342
08:34:35,040 --> 08:34:39,040
Hezbollah in Lebanon, the Houthis in Yemen,

7343
08:34:39,040 --> 08:34:42,040
whether they fit into this into this frame as well,

7344
08:34:42,040 --> 08:34:47,040
because I want to know if they are motivated by the same impulses

7345
08:34:47,040 --> 08:34:53,040
that perhaps Iran or the United States may be for their own ends.

7346
08:34:53,040 --> 08:34:56,040
I mean, how does your approach to this work,

7347
08:34:56,040 --> 08:35:01,040
when war is being waged by smaller paramilitary networks,

7348
08:35:01,040 --> 08:35:06,040
okay, some see Hezbollah as being almost a kind of mini-franchise of Iran

7349
08:35:06,040 --> 08:35:10,040
in the same way that you would view the branch of a bank internationally.

7350
08:35:10,040 --> 08:35:15,040
But is that a wrong way of looking at this?

7351
08:35:15,040 --> 08:35:21,190
Well, I think a lot of these non-state actors,

7352
08:35:21,190 --> 08:35:26,190
they do very often have state backers,

7353
08:35:26,190 --> 08:35:29,190
and we've seen it with Hezbollah,

7354
08:35:29,190 --> 08:35:37,190
we've seen it with the rapid support forces and the UAE in Sudan.

7355
08:35:37,190 --> 08:35:43,190
So we can't sort of regard them simply as non-state actors.

7356
08:35:43,190 --> 08:35:48,190
I think that in the case of Hezbollah in Lebanon,

7357
08:35:48,190 --> 08:35:53,190
there has been quite a strong effort from the Lebanese government

7358
08:35:53,190 --> 08:35:56,190
to weaken Hezbollah,

7359
08:35:56,190 --> 08:36:01,190
and that's something that can potentially, I think, bear a lot of fruit.

7360
08:36:01,190 --> 08:36:07,190
And then when Israel comes in and starts bombing Lebanon

7361
08:36:07,190 --> 08:36:13,190
and displacing, as I understand it, a million people,

7362
08:36:13,190 --> 08:36:17,420
this can easily set things back.

7363
08:36:17,420 --> 08:36:22,420
So the states tend to sort of weigh in on these struggles,

7364
08:36:22,420 --> 08:36:27,420
these local struggles, often in extremely counterproductive ways.

7365
08:36:27,420 --> 08:36:34,420
I think Sudan is an interesting case of a government that,

7366
08:36:34,420 --> 08:36:39,420
going back to the 1980s, tried to mobilize militias,

7367
08:36:39,420 --> 08:36:44,510
mobilize its own militias for the purposes of counterinsurgency.

7368
08:36:44,510 --> 08:36:48,510
But then the government, in a way, lost control of the violence

7369
08:36:48,510 --> 08:36:53,510
because the militias were economically motivated to a large extent.

7370
08:36:53,510 --> 08:36:56,510
They spread rebellion to new areas,

7371
08:36:56,510 --> 08:37:01,510
and on the whole, they refused to disband after particular military campaigns,

7372
08:37:01,510 --> 08:37:06,510
and they became, as were, a political factor in their own right.

7373
08:37:06,510 --> 08:37:13,510
And we know that the RSF is a successor to the Janjaweed militias

7374
08:37:13,510 --> 08:37:16,510
that created the Darfur genocide.

7375
08:37:16,510 --> 08:37:22,510
And it's been a huge factor in Sudan politics ever since,

7376
08:37:22,510 --> 08:37:25,510
basically extorting the government,

7377
08:37:25,510 --> 08:37:29,510
using gold in particular to build up its strength,

7378
08:37:29,510 --> 08:37:32,510
and eventually turning against the army

7379
08:37:32,510 --> 08:37:37,510
with the result of this horrific civil war that we have at the moment.

7380
08:37:37,510 --> 08:37:42,510
So militias don't always obey what their puppet masters

7381
08:37:42,510 --> 08:37:47,510
tell them to do, and that can be very destructive in itself.

7382
08:37:47,510 --> 08:37:52,510
David, can a conflict like the Iran-Israel rivalry ever truly end,

7383
08:37:52,510 --> 08:37:54,510
taking in everything you've discussed,

7384
08:37:54,510 --> 08:38:00,510
or does that system simply evolve into new forms of hybrid warfare,

7385
08:38:00,510 --> 08:38:03,510
or to protect essentially underlying profit margins

7386
08:38:03,510 --> 08:38:05,510
and existing power structures?

7387
08:38:05,510 --> 08:38:10,670
Well, I think conflicts can come to an end.

7388
08:38:11,670 --> 08:38:16,670
I think a lot of people thought that the Cold War would never end

7389
08:38:16,670 --> 08:38:20,670
because partly of the vested interests unfold,

7390
08:38:20,670 --> 08:38:23,670
but it did actually surprise us by ending.

7391
08:38:23,670 --> 08:38:28,670
Of course, people who were making the argument that it will never end

7392
08:38:28,670 --> 08:38:34,670
will now point to various aspects of a revival of the Cold War,

7393
08:38:34,670 --> 08:38:39,670
in terms of Russia and even China and so on.

7394
08:38:39,670 --> 08:38:44,670
But I do think sometimes that violence is a little bit like energy

7395
08:38:44,670 --> 08:38:48,700
in the sense that it can change from one form to another,

7396
08:38:48,700 --> 08:38:51,700
but it very rarely disappears entirely.

7397
08:38:51,700 --> 08:38:57,700
In practice, I think one of the things you can do is to make peace

7398
08:38:57,700 --> 08:39:02,700
more profitable and more attractive for various warring parties

7399
08:39:02,700 --> 08:39:04,700
than war itself.

7400
08:39:04,700 --> 08:39:08,700
You can offer them a stake in a political settlement, in a peace process.

7401
08:39:08,700 --> 08:39:11,700
You can buy them off if you like.

7402
08:39:11,700 --> 08:39:17,740
One of the problems with that, of course, is that you tend to entrench corruption

7403
08:39:17,740 --> 08:39:22,740
and indeed violence within the peace settlement

7404
08:39:22,740 --> 08:39:26,740
that you're actually creating or offering.

7405
08:39:26,740 --> 08:39:29,740
David, in a fragmented global economy,

7406
08:39:29,740 --> 08:39:34,740
how does the political conflict of those big powers,

7407
08:39:34,740 --> 08:39:37,740
the political conflict that we are seeing play out in the Middle East,

7408
08:39:37,740 --> 08:39:40,740
I mean, how does that affect the global south?

7409
08:39:40,740 --> 08:39:44,740
How does that affect nations that are watching from the sidelines

7410
08:39:44,740 --> 08:39:48,740
or simultaneously protecting the big powers in Washington D.C.

7411
08:39:48,740 --> 08:39:52,740
in Beijing or Moscow, for instance?

7412
08:39:52,740 --> 08:39:57,800
I think there are a lot of economic impacts as well.

7413
08:39:57,800 --> 08:39:59,800
For example, there were...

7414
08:40:00,000 --> 08:40:05,000
Food Program now is talking about perhaps 45 million people

7415
08:40:07,000 --> 08:40:10,880
facing severe food shortages and starvation

7416
08:40:10,880 --> 08:40:14,600
as a result of the war that's currently going on

7417
08:40:14,600 --> 08:40:18,900
with Iran and Israel and the United States.

7418
08:40:18,900 --> 08:40:23,120
So, you know, we know that oil prices are going up.

7419
08:40:23,120 --> 08:40:25,760
We know that fertilizer prices

7420
08:40:25,760 --> 08:40:28,360
have been drastically affected.

7421
08:40:28,360 --> 08:40:30,240
This affects food production

7422
08:40:30,240 --> 08:40:33,040
and it affects people's food security.

7423
08:40:33,040 --> 08:40:35,680
And I think, you know, if we go back in history

7424
08:40:35,680 --> 08:40:40,150
just a little bit to, for example, 2011,

7425
08:40:40,150 --> 08:40:44,350
you know, you had a huge increase in global food prices

7426
08:40:44,350 --> 08:40:46,110
for various reasons.

7427
08:40:46,110 --> 08:40:49,030
And that fed rather directly into famine

7428
08:40:49,030 --> 08:40:52,950
in places like Yemen and Somalia.

7429
08:40:52,950 --> 08:40:55,730
And it also fed into the so-called Arab Spring

7430
08:40:55,730 --> 08:40:59,850
and the Civil War in Syria, for example.

7431
08:40:59,850 --> 08:41:02,170
So these kind of knock-on effects, you know,

7432
08:41:02,170 --> 08:41:04,290
they're not always predictable,

7433
08:41:04,290 --> 08:41:07,170
but I think you can predict that many of them

7434
08:41:07,170 --> 08:41:09,090
are going to be extremely bad

7435
08:41:09,090 --> 08:41:11,410
and the people that are going to suffer

7436
08:41:11,410 --> 08:41:16,250
are going to be, you know, very far from Washington

7437
08:41:16,250 --> 08:41:18,650
or Jerusalem.

7438
08:41:18,650 --> 08:41:22,160
David King, thank you for talking to Al Jazeera.

7439
08:41:22,160 --> 08:41:35,070
Yeah, pleasure to talk to you.

7440
08:41:35,070 --> 08:41:36,870
As the war on Iran escalates,

7441
08:41:36,870 --> 08:41:39,510
Al Jazeera's correspondents are on the ground.

7442
08:41:39,510 --> 08:41:42,110
Donald Trump says he intends to speak to the Iranians

7443
08:41:42,110 --> 08:41:43,270
in the next couple of days,

7444
08:41:43,270 --> 08:41:44,670
but added he has no idea

7445
08:41:44,670 --> 08:41:46,230
who will be on the other end of the call.

7446
08:41:46,230 --> 08:41:48,270
Well, most of the people we had in mind are dead.

7447
08:41:48,270 --> 08:41:50,990
A lot of signs, people saying that the U.S.

7448
08:41:50,990 --> 08:41:52,830
should not be attacking Iran,

7449
08:41:52,830 --> 08:41:54,470
calling on Congress to act.

7450
08:41:54,470 --> 08:41:57,590
Members of Congress are away from Washington this weekend.

7451
08:41:57,590 --> 08:41:59,470
There needs to be the proof

7452
08:41:59,470 --> 08:42:02,710
of an imminent threat to American interest.

7453
08:42:02,710 --> 08:42:05,630
This was our last best chance to strike.

7454
08:42:05,630 --> 08:42:08,670
The U.S. Senate has decided they are not going to step in

7455
08:42:08,670 --> 08:42:11,110
and stop this ever-expanding war.

7456
08:42:11,110 --> 08:42:14,750
Stay with Al Jazeera for the latest developments.

7457
08:42:14,750 --> 08:42:17,550
Tehran, a city where centuries of history

7458
08:42:17,550 --> 08:42:20,030
rise from its histories and monuments,

7459
08:42:20,030 --> 08:42:21,950
now coming under assault.

7460
08:42:21,950 --> 08:42:25,110
At the heart of the city stands Kolesan Palace.

7461
08:42:25,110 --> 08:42:27,150
More than four centuries old,

7462
08:42:27,150 --> 08:42:30,790
a witness to coronations and major political decisions,

7463
08:42:30,790 --> 08:42:34,310
but now damaged by American and Israeli airstrikes.

7464
08:42:34,310 --> 08:42:36,630
International organizations like UNESCO

7465
08:42:36,630 --> 08:42:38,950
warn about the impact of these attacks

7466
08:42:38,950 --> 08:42:40,630
on our collective heritage.

7467
08:42:40,630 --> 08:42:43,950
They are UNESCO World Heritage sites,

7468
08:42:43,950 --> 08:42:48,470
priceless treasures that belong to all of humanity.

7469
08:42:48,470 --> 08:42:51,390
Many now fear that centuries of history

7470
08:42:51,390 --> 08:43:12,760
could become another casualty of war.

7471
08:43:12,760 --> 08:43:14,800
Hello, this is Al Jazeera live from Doha

7472
08:43:14,800 --> 08:43:16,560
with our continuing coverage of the war

7473
08:43:16,560 --> 08:43:19,120
between the U.S., Israel and Iran.

7474
08:43:19,120 --> 08:43:21,820
I'm Riley Carlson, here's what's coming up.

7475
08:43:21,820 --> 08:43:24,500
Donald Trump sets a Tuesday deadline for Iran

7476
08:43:24,500 --> 08:43:26,380
to reopen the Strait of Hormuz

7477
08:43:26,380 --> 08:43:29,260
or the U.S. will bomb its power plants, bridges,

7478
08:43:29,260 --> 08:43:32,470
and quote, unleash hell.

7479
08:43:32,470 --> 08:43:35,870
We are determined to defend.

7480
08:43:35,870 --> 08:43:37,790
Iran's foreign ministry dismisses

7481
08:43:37,790 --> 08:43:39,230
the U.S. president's warning

7482
08:43:39,230 --> 08:43:44,180
and vows a swift retaliation to any U.S. attacks.

7483
08:43:44,180 --> 08:43:47,300
That is airstrike slam into Tehran province.

7484
08:43:47,300 --> 08:43:49,300
At least 23 people have been killed,

7485
08:43:49,340 --> 08:43:52,440
including six children.

7486
08:43:52,440 --> 08:43:54,200
At least five people have been killed

7487
08:43:54,200 --> 08:43:57,200
in an Israeli strike on Lebanon's capital.

7488
08:43:57,200 --> 08:44:04,520
We're live in Beirut's southern suburb.

7489
08:44:04,520 --> 08:44:07,760
And more Iranian attacks target Israeli cities.

7490
08:44:07,760 --> 08:44:09,240
Two bodies have been recovered

7491
08:44:09,240 --> 08:44:21,780
from the rubble of a destroyed apartment building in Haifa.

7492
08:44:21,780 --> 08:44:24,420
U.S. president Donald Trump has renewed threats

7493
08:44:24,420 --> 08:44:27,220
to destroy Iranian power plants and bridges

7494
08:44:27,220 --> 08:44:29,780
if it doesn't reopen the Strait of Hormuz.

7495
08:44:29,780 --> 08:44:33,220
He shared what appears to be a deadline on social media.

7496
08:44:33,220 --> 08:44:36,180
Tuesday, 8 p.m. Eastern time.

7497
08:44:36,180 --> 08:44:38,820
Earlier, Trump posted an expletive latent threat

7498
08:44:38,820 --> 08:44:40,780
to his true social account.

7499
08:44:40,780 --> 08:44:42,980
It promised that Iranians would be quote,

7500
08:44:42,980 --> 08:44:46,640
living in hell if the Strait isn't reopened.

7501
08:44:46,640 --> 08:44:49,960
Tehran has emphasized that it will retaliate if struck.

7502
08:44:49,960 --> 08:44:52,280
Foreign ministry spokesman Ismail Bagai

7503
08:44:52,280 --> 08:44:54,520
says any U.S. related location

7504
08:44:54,520 --> 08:44:57,730
in the Middle East would be a target.

7505
08:44:57,730 --> 08:44:59,610
Our armed forces have made it clear

7506
08:44:59,610 --> 08:45:04,610
that in case Iran's infrastructure is attacked,

7507
08:45:06,170 --> 08:45:08,930
we would react in kind.

7508
08:45:08,930 --> 08:45:11,410
They would, our armed forces,

7509
08:45:11,410 --> 08:45:15,210
would target any similar infrastructure

7510
08:45:15,210 --> 08:45:18,330
that is owned or in any way or manner

7511
08:45:18,330 --> 08:45:20,330
related to the United States

7512
08:45:21,370 --> 08:45:26,080
or contributes to their act of aggression against Iran.

7513
08:45:26,080 --> 08:45:28,200
We will be live from Tehran in just a moment,

7514
08:45:28,200 --> 08:45:30,040
but first, Al Jazeera's Mike Hanna

7515
08:45:30,040 --> 08:45:34,550
has more on the president's comments from Washington, D.C.

7516
08:45:34,550 --> 08:45:38,310
The unusual use of an expletive by a national leader,

7517
08:45:38,310 --> 08:45:42,030
perhaps a symbol of President Trump's frustration

7518
08:45:42,030 --> 08:45:43,750
with the Iranian government

7519
08:45:43,750 --> 08:45:46,390
and their refusal to concede to the demands

7520
08:45:46,390 --> 08:45:47,550
that he is making.

7521
08:45:47,550 --> 08:45:50,430
That reference as well was to the Strait of Hormuz,

7522
08:45:50,430 --> 08:45:53,110
which in recent days, President Trump had insisted

7523
08:45:53,110 --> 08:45:55,150
he did not care about saying the U.S.

7524
08:45:55,150 --> 08:45:57,790
did not need the oil passing through the Strait

7525
08:45:57,790 --> 08:46:00,830
and it was up to the countries who are reliant on that oil

7526
08:46:00,830 --> 08:46:02,190
to deal with the situation.

7527
08:46:02,190 --> 08:46:05,430
But now the demand about opening the Strait of Hormuz

7528
08:46:05,430 --> 08:46:08,230
appears front and center of the president's

7529
08:46:08,230 --> 08:46:11,510
new demands of the Iranian government.

7530
08:46:11,510 --> 08:46:14,950
President Trump threatening to, and I use his words,

7531
08:46:14,950 --> 08:46:18,230
blowing everything and taking the oil

7532
08:46:18,230 --> 08:46:21,190
if Iran does not meet his new deadline,

7533
08:46:21,190 --> 08:46:24,310
which has now been set at 8 p.m. Eastern time

7534
08:46:24,310 --> 08:46:25,990
on Tuesday evening.

7535
08:46:25,990 --> 08:46:28,190
He says in an earlier media post

7536
08:46:28,190 --> 08:46:31,350
that a power plant day and bridge day

7537
08:46:31,350 --> 08:46:33,630
all wrapped up on Tuesday.

7538
08:46:33,630 --> 08:46:36,190
But on the other hand, in addition to these threats,

7539
08:46:36,190 --> 08:46:38,030
President Trump is sending signals

7540
08:46:38,030 --> 08:46:41,270
that there's some kind of negotiation process underway.

7541
08:46:41,270 --> 08:46:43,990
He's indicated that his close confidants,

7542
08:46:43,990 --> 08:46:46,550
Jared Kushner and Stephen Whitcoff,

7543
08:46:46,550 --> 08:46:49,870
are in consultation with either the Iranian government

7544
08:46:49,870 --> 08:46:53,270
and identified figures or some form of intermediary.

7545
08:46:53,270 --> 08:46:55,870
And speaking to U.S. news outlets,

7546
08:46:55,870 --> 08:46:58,630
President Trump says that he is optimistic

7547
08:46:58,630 --> 08:47:01,190
that there can be an agreement very soon.

7548
08:47:01,190 --> 08:47:05,150
He says the agreement could come within a matter of days

7549
08:47:05,150 --> 08:47:07,710
rather than a matter of weeks.

7550
08:47:07,710 --> 08:47:09,710
So you've got this dichotomous approach

7551
08:47:09,710 --> 08:47:11,750
from the president on the one hand,

7552
08:47:11,750 --> 08:47:16,390
threatening obliteration to civilian targets within Iran,

7553
08:47:16,390 --> 08:47:19,830
which some observers would contend to be a war crime.

7554
08:47:19,830 --> 08:47:22,190
But on the other hand, what he is saying is,

7555
08:47:22,190 --> 08:47:25,110
